DIAGRAMMATIC AND PICTORIAL REASONING 
Leibniz characteristica - calculus ratiocinator

AIM
Characters that can be seen as well as calculated.
A diagram shows a logical relation so an operation can be done by eye,
without first turning it into speech.

THREE FACES OF ONE RELATION
  verbal     Every man is an animal
  numeric    2 | 6
  pictorial  the smaller mark sits inside the larger one
If the picture is faithful, moving the marks is already reasoning.

FUNDAMENTALS
  P = {p1, p2, ..., pn}     alphabet of primitive concepts
  A subseteq P, A nonempty  compound term / complexion
  char(T)                   characteristic number of term T
  S in Q                    S contains Q   (inesse)
  AB                        conceptual conjunction (product)
  ~A                        conceptual negation
  gcd(a,b)=1                coprime pair in the 1679 model

TYPES
  Primitive     undecomposed mark          p, animal=2, rational=3
  Compound      product / subset           man = animal * rational = 6
  Subject S     term under test
  Predicate Q   term claimed of S
  Pair term     <+ t1 | - t2 >             positives / privatives
  Diagram       visible relation among marks
  Operation     containment, product, overlap, exclusion

VARIABLES
  n             number of primitives
  k             size of a combination
  S, Q, T       terms
  s = char(S)   number of the subject
  q = char(Q)   number of the predicate
  <s, sigma>    pair for S
  <p, pi>       pair for Q
  A, B          subsets of P

CORE EQUATIONS
  Pred(S)  = { A subseteq S : A nonempty }          |Pred(S)|  = 2^|S| - 1
  Subj(Q)  = { B subseteq P : Q subseteq B }        |Subj(Q)|  = 2^{n-|Q|}
  S in Q   <=>  Q subseteq S  <=>  q | s
  char(AB) = char(A) * char(B)
  All S are Q                 p|s  and  pi|sigma
  No S is Q                   gcd(s,pi)>1  or  gcd(p,sigma)>1
  Some S is Q                 gcd(s,pi)=gcd(p,sigma)=1
  Some S is not Q             p does not divide s  or  pi does not divide sigma

CONTAINMENT PICTURE
  Universal affirmative: Q is in S

          ======= S =======
             === Q ===

  Test:  Q inside S  <=>  Q subseteq S  <=>  char(Q)|char(S)

COMBINATION PICTURE
  primitives on a line

          p1   p2   p3   p4   p5
          *    *    *    *    *

  product

          [animal] * [rational]  -->  [man]
              2    *     3       -->    6

  tree

                    man
                   /   \
              animal   rational
                 |         |
                 2         3

FOUR CATEGORICAL PICTURES
  A  All S are P         P inside S
        ==== S ====
          == P ==

  E  No S is P           disjoint
        [ S ]     [ P ]

  I  Some S is P         overlap
        S [##] P
            shared marks

  O  Some S is not P     S not swallowed by P
        [ S ] --x-- [ P ]

